lisa-design:
- ByControl's ?downstreamSspId= is a CONVENIENCE FILTER, not an authorization boundary. No
  permission is evaluated against the SSP it names. It cannot surface an offering the caller could
  not already list (it only removes rows), but it does let a catalog reader infer an offering's
  allow-list membership by diffing filtered vs unfiltered responses. ACCEPTED, explicitly, not by
  omission: allow-list membership (who may leverage whom) is non-confidential topology, and a
  per-request PDP round-trip on a read path is not worth paying to hide it. Documented in the
  handler godoc. If it must become confidential, evaluate ssp:read on downstreamSspId the way
  Subscribe does.
- The shared-responsibility `legacy` arm reports ONLY requirement-anchored by-components that
  actually carry shared responsibility (an export, an inherited, or a satisfied row). A bare
  requirement-anchored by-component is the ordinary OSCAL shape for "this component implements this
  control" — every imported SSP is full of them — and reporting those as debt would invite users to
  delete their own control implementation. Reason is "requirement-anchored export", or
  "requirement-anchored inherited/satisfied" when it carries consumer-side rows but no export.

lisa-addressed:
Human review round 2 (gusfcarvalho, PR #456) — 6 further findings, all valid, all fixed:

- [Medium] legacy arm over-reported: it appended EVERY requirement-anchored by-component, so any
  normally-imported SSP had its entire control implementation returned as "requirement-anchored
  export" debt. The test fixture pinned the bug (a bare ByComponent with no export). FIXED: only
  report when the by-component carries an export / inherited / satisfied row; reason distinguishes
  the two cases. Fixture now seeds a real export, plus a bare one asserted NOT to be reported.
- [Low] Subscribe derived satisfaction from a narrower input (this request's satisfiedSet) than
  every reader (all satisfied rows on the by-component), so a pre-existing hand-authored satisfied
  row made the STORED value disagree with the projection — and the stored value is what the drift
  detector consumes. FIXED: Subscribe now calls resyncLeverageSatisfaction after creating each link,
  and re-reads the links before responding, so "derived in exactly one place" is literally true.
- [Low] Inherited POST validated that provided-uuid PARSES but not that it RESOLVES, creating inert
  rows that can never have a satisfied entry accepted against them. FIXED: 400 when it doesn't
  resolve, mirroring the satisfied POST. Two existing fixtures had to stop inventing provided-uuids.
- [Low] ByControl's ?downstreamSspId= leaks allow-list membership. Reviewer offered "authz or
  document it". DOCUMENTED as an explicit, accepted trade-off (see design note).
- [Low] TestSharedResponsibilityFiltersByControl's helper dropped the status assertion its sibling
  has, so an error body unmarshalled to a zero-value rollup and its three negative assertions passed
  on a 500. FIXED: assert 200.
- [Low] The route-absence loop asserted PUT/DELETE against a collection path no router would ever
  register, so it would keep passing if someone added the real item routes. FIXED: assert all four
  methods against BOTH the collection and the :inheritedId/:satisfiedId item shapes.
